𝟑𝟎 𝐃𝐚𝐲𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 – 𝐃𝐚𝐲 𝟏𝟖/𝟑𝟎

🔥 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐬 – 𝐑𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐫𝐭 𝐒𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐜𝐞𝐬 𝐎𝐧𝐥𝐲 𝐖𝐡𝐞𝐧 𝐍𝐞𝐞𝐝𝐞𝐝

Imagine managing 100 production servers and updating an Nginx configuration.

Would you restart Nginx on every server?

❌ No. That would be unnecessary.

This is where 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐬 become useful. 🚀

🧩 𝐖𝐡𝐚𝐭 𝐢𝐬 𝐚𝐧 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫?

A handler is a special task that runs only when another task reports a change.

Common uses:

🔄 Restart a service
♻️ Reload a service
⚙️ Apply configuration changes
🚀 Restart an application

Handlers work with the 𝐧𝐨𝐭𝐢𝐟𝐲 keyword.

⚙️ 𝐁𝐚𝐬𝐢𝐜 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

- name: Deploy Nginx configuration
  template:
    src: nginx.conf.j2
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  notify: Restart Nginx

handlers:
  - name: Restart Nginx
    service:
      name: nginx
      state: restarted

The flow:

Configuration changes → notify → Handler → Nginx restarts

If nothing changes:

No change → No handler → No restart. ✅

🔥 𝐖𝐡𝐲 𝐃𝐨 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐬 𝐌𝐚𝐭𝐭𝐞𝐫?

This is closely related to 𝐢𝐝𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐨𝐭𝐞𝐧𝐜𝐲.

Run your playbook 5 times with no configuration changes:

❌ Don't restart Nginx 5 times.

Instead:

✅ No change
✅ No handler execution
✅ No unnecessary restart

🏗️ 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

If you manage 100 web servers and only 20 need configuration changes:

🟢 20 servers → Changed → Restarted

⚪ 80 servers → No change → No restart

That's the behavior we want in production.

⏱️ 𝐖𝐡𝐞𝐧 𝐃𝐨 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐬 𝐑𝐮𝐧?

By default, handlers run at the end of the play.

Multiple tasks can notify the same handler, but Ansible normally executes it only once per play.

⚠️ 𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐨𝐧 𝐌𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐬

❌ Incorrect handler name
❌ Forgetting notify
❌ Restarting services unnecessarily
❌ Using restart when reload is sufficient
❌ Assuming handlers run immediately

💡 𝐏𝐫𝐨𝐝𝐮𝐜𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐌𝐢𝐧𝐝𝐬𝐞𝐭

A good DevOps Engineer doesn't just ask:

"Can I automate this?"

Ask:

"Can I automate this safely and efficiently?"

🎯 𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐓𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐚𝐰𝐚𝐲

𝐓𝐚𝐬𝐤𝐬 𝐦𝐚𝐤𝐞 𝐜𝐡𝐚𝐧𝐠𝐞𝐬.
𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫𝐬 𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐜𝐭 𝐭𝐨 𝐭𝐡𝐨𝐬𝐞 𝐜𝐡𝐚𝐧𝐠𝐞𝐬.

𝐂𝐡𝐚𝐧𝐠𝐞 → 𝐧𝐨𝐭𝐢𝐟𝐲 → 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐫 → 𝐑𝐞𝐥𝐨𝐚𝐝/𝐑𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐫𝐭 🚀
